<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kazakhstan</h1><p class="st-meta">1991 CE – present · capital Astana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The world's largest landlocked country, a resource-rich steppe state that built a new capital at Astana and balances between Russia, China and the West.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kazakh-ssr">Kazakh SSR</a> was the last Soviet republic to declare independence, on 16 December 1991, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nursultan-nazarbayev">Nursultan Nazarbayev</a>, who had led it since 1989. The new state inherited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semey">Semipalatinsk</a> test site, which was closed, and a large nuclear arsenal, which it gave up by 1995.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nursultan-nazarbayev">Nazarbayev</a> ruled as president for nearly three decades, building a strong presidential system with little political competition. Oil from fields such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tengiz-field">Tengiz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashagan-field">Kashagan</a>, gas, uranium and metals brought rapid growth. The capital moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Almaty</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astana">Astana</a> in 1997. The state promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakh</a> language and culture while keeping Russian widely used and managing a large ethnic Russian minority in the north.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nursultan-nazarbayev">Nazarbayev</a> resigned in 2019 in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassym-jomart-tokayev">Kassym-Jomart Tokayev</a> but kept influence until the January 2022 unrest, when protests over fuel prices turned violent and Russian-led troops were briefly deployed. Tokayev then curbed Nazarbayev's family and introduced constitutional changes.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kazakhstan.jpg" alt="Kazakhstan" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Shaken Niyazbekop · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kazakhstan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1991 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Astana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nursultan Nazarbayev, Kassym-Jomart Tokayev</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Russian Orthodoxy</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kazakh, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Kazakhstan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=kazakhstan" aria-label="Kazakhstan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6302 405 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kazakhstan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6622 661L6583 753"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6583 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6622 661h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6622 661h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Astana. <a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=kazakhstan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
